35,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"My findings reveal a significant trade-off. While hardware-based holdings allow for independent verification of figures, they impose a heavy burden on our quarter-end, requiring costly outside appraisals and extending our audit timelines by six weeks.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My findings suggest a mismatch in our human capacity. While we have a surplus of software expertise, our partners are overextended. Conversely, we have logistics experts available, but finding operational supervisors takes far too long.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"My findings suggest that our success has come from a very specific place. Our profitable exits were quiet back-office tools, while our write-off involved the complexity of physical premises and hourly crews.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"The financial reality is concerning. Our holdings are calling follow-on capital at twice the expected rate, and our credit line provides no value against subscription contracts. We are far more liquidity-constrained than we have admitted.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Shion,,"The data confirms a pattern of systemic friction. Security reviews for sensitive data are agonizingly slow, and hardware dependencies create unpredictable delays. We must avoid any candidate whose deployment relies on physical supply chains or heavy regulation.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Shion, your observation on hardware delays aligns with my findings on audit burdens. It seems the very assets that provide verifiable data also create the most friction in our reporting and closing processes.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Mizuki, it is a quiet tragedy when the tools meant to provide clarity instead create chaos. It seems the physical world brings a restlessness to our operations that we may not have the capacity to manage.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Jonathan, that is a very gentle way of putting it. I also found that our most successful paths were the quietest ones—simple tools that customers truly valued and kept, even when prices rose.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Shizue, simplicity is a necessity, not just a preference. If we are calling follow-on capital at double the rate, we cannot afford the volatility of physical assets or complex operations. We need a lean model.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Shion,,"@Misaki, ""lean"" is a vague term. What we actually need is a path devoid of external bottlenecks. Whether it is a controller shortage or a regulatory review, the result is a stalled deployment. We must prioritize predictability over narrative.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@Shion, I agree. Predictability is essential, especially when our quarter-end is already strained. If we add a holding that requires external appraisals and extends our audit by six weeks, we are simply inviting more chaos.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Mizuki, I agree. If we choose a path that demands people we cannot find or partners who are already exhausted, we sacrifice our peace. We should seek a candidate that fits our current capacity.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Jonathan, I agree completely. It would be so unkind to push our partners to their limits. We should look for a candidate that brings a sense of calm and stability to our existing team.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Shizue, stability is only possible if we are solvent. If we invest in a company that triggers unexpected capital calls, we risk the entire fund's health. We must prioritize a candidate with minimal follow-on requirements.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Shion,,"@Misaki, solvency is a baseline, not a strategy. While you worry about the bank account, I am looking at the clock. A lean company that spends a year in security review is just a slow drain on our capital. We need a clear path to the first customer.","{""words"":48,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"It seems we are all describing the same systemic fragility from different angles. Whether it is liquidity, staffing, or audit timelines, the common enemy is operational complexity. We must prioritize a candidate that simplifies, rather than complicates, our reporting.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree, Mizuki. When we align our ambitions with our actual capacity, we find a path to true serenity. Let us seek a candidate that requires neither scarce specialists nor the time of our exhausted partners.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Shizue,,"It is so heartening to see us all reaching a common understanding. Let us look for a candidate that is gentle on our resources and honest in its value, ensuring a peaceful journey for everyone involved.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Misaki,,"I agree. We must be cold-headed about this. The ideal candidate is one that avoids physical assets and heavy capital demands, ensuring we don't deplete our reserves faster than we can replenish them.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Shion,,"We have successfully identified the variables that lead to failure. The ideal candidate is not the most ambitious, but the most unobstructed. I shall be looking for the one with the fewest external dependencies.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
